 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Sharp Feature Rendering via Clip Lines
-A technique to render sharp features (edges, corners) in point-sampled surfaces by explicitly representing features as clip lines in local tangent planes [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by discarding parts of reconstruction kernels to prevent blurring, rather than integrating restricted densities. It is chosen when rendering point-sampled objects like cubes to avoid geometric artifacts. It uses clip lines defined by two homogeneous points $c_h, d_h$ in the local tangent plane, projected to image space as $c', d'$. A direction vector $v$ perpendicular to the line is computed. The kernel is evaluated at $x$ if $(x - c') \cdot v > 0$ [S2, unofficial]. Parameter: Fg (cutoff value, e.g., 0.5) [S2, unofficial].
+A technique to render sharp features (edges, corners) in point-sampled surfaces by explicitly representing features as clip lines in local tangent planes [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by discarding parts of reconstruction kernels to prevent blurring, rather than integrating restricted densities [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when rendering point-sampled objects like cubes to avoid geometric artifacts [S2, unofficial]. It uses clip lines defined by two homogeneous points $c_h, d_h$ in the local tangent plane, projected to image space as $c', d'$ [S2, unofficial]. A direction vector $v$ perpendicular to the line is computed [S2, unofficial]. The kernel is evaluated at $x$ if $(x - c') \cdot v > 0$ [S2, unofficial]. Parameter: Fg (cutoff value, e.g., 0.5) [S2, unofficial].
 
